ility at least $1/(4\kappa)$; root finding~\cite{Rabin1980} is called only once, on the accepted $u$, where $X^\kappa - u$ has $\kappa$ distinct roots in $\Fp$. One splitting attempt on a factor of degree $n \le \kappa$ computes $(X+\delta)^{(p-1)/2}$ modulo that factor with $O(n^2b)$ operations and a gcd. It separates two given roots $r_1 \ne r_2$ with probability at least $(p-1)/(2p) \ge 1/3$, since $\chi((r_1+\delta)(r_2+\delta)) = 1$, $\chi$ the quadratic character, for only $(p-3)/2$ values of $\delta$, and the two roots stay together for at most two further values ($\delta = -r_1, -r_2$). Keeping the smaller factor at least halves the degree, so one root costs $O(\kappa^2b)$ expected operations. In every case the expected cost is $O(\kappa^2 b)$ field operations, $O(b^2)$ steps each, and head movement over the at most $\kappa+1$ coefficients adds a factor polynomial in $\kappa$; this is the bound $E_d$ of Remark~\ref{rem:is-runtime}. Counting all of this, together with the Bezout step of Remark~\ref{rem:is-import}(b), gives at most $40\kappa^3 N_{\mathrm{col}} b(a^2+b^2)$ multiplications and inversions in $\Fp$ and at most twice as many additions, where $N_{\mathrm{col}} = \mathcal{G}^{L} \le M_d$; at $\kappa_d = 1$ the count is $a^2(a+1)+2a+2b(a+1)$. With schoolbook arithmetic and the binary extended Euclidean algorithm, each of these operations costs $O(b^2)$ steps of a multitape Turing machine that keeps its operands and $p$ on dedicated tapes.
